 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds $O(k^2)$ per pixel per channel for convolution, where $k$ is the kernel size. Since $k$ is small (e.g., 9x9) and decreases, the overhead is negligible compared to the rendering cost.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the blurred image or recomputing it on the fly. No additional large data structures.
-*   **Measured Performance:** [S3] reports that Adaptive Gaussian Blur improves PSNR from 19.54 (Baseline) to 19.72 on DTU 3-view, with SSIM improving from 0.766 to 0.771 and LPIPS from 0.221 to 0.218.
-*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment [S2, unofficial]; restricts high-frequency noise in the learned scene [S2, unofficial]; reduces training iterations required for convergence [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Handcrafted schedule [S3]; cannot fully align frequency relationships between positional encoding and pixel supervision as effectively as adaptive methods [S3].
-*   **When to Use:** Early stages of joint optimization, especially when camera poses are being refined [S1]. Crucial for robustness in pose refinement [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Late-stage training where high-frequency details are needed; if an adaptive frequency-aware loss is available, it may be superior [S3].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Time: Adding a Gaussian blur step increases per-iteration cost by $O(W^2)$ per pixel, where $W$ is the kernel size. Using separable convolution reduces this to $O(W)$ per pixel [S1].
+- Memory: Requires storing the blurred images or computing them on-the-fly. On-the-fly computation saves memory [G].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- On DTU 3-view, Smoothed 2D Supervision (Adaptive Gaussian Blur) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the Baseline (19.54) [S3].
+- It reduces training iterations to 50k compared to 200k for previous MLP-based methods, reducing overall training time to 25% [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- Strengths: Stabilizes camera alignment, restricts high-frequency noise, and improves robustness in joint optimization [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- Limitations: Handcrafted schedule may not fully align frequency relationships compared to adaptive methods like [FreGS](9212-fregs.md) [S3].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when joint optimization of camera poses and scene parameters is unstable [S1].
+- Use when high-frequency noise in training images leads to poor convergence [S2, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+- When adaptive frequency alignment is available and preferred [S3].
+- When computational budget is extremely tight and blur cost is prohibitive [G].
 
 ## Background
-Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced as part of techniques to improve the robustness of joint optimization for camera poses and radiance fields [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was inspired by spectral analysis showing that blurring supervision images produces smoother gradients [S2, unofficial]. The method is analogous to the Gaussian schedule used in 3D radiance fields (NeRF) but applied to the 2D image space [S2, unofficial]. It has been adopted in various forms in Gaussian Splatting variants (e.g., Trick-GS [S4]) and NeRF regularization (e.g., Few-shot NeRF [S3]).
+Smoothed 2D Supervision was introduced to improve the robustness of joint optimization in radiance fields and Gaussian Splatting [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is inspired by spectral analysis showing that blurring training images produces smoother gradients and restricts the spectrum of the learned scene [S2, unofficial]. It is often used in conjunction with other regularization techniques like [Frequency Regularization](6130-frequency-regularization.md) and [Randomly Scaled Kernel Parameter](7875-randomly-scaled-kernel-parameter.md) [S1].
 
